---
import ProsePage from '../../templates/ProsePage.astro';
import { routePath } from '../../i18n/routes.ts';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, REPO_URL } from '../../config/site.ts';
---

<ProsePage
  locale="en"
  routeKey="about"
  title="About twente.dev"
  description="What it is, who runs it, who pays for it, and what you can expect. Short, and checkable."
>
  <p>
    twente.dev is a calendar, an evening and an archive for people who build technology in Twente.
    One person runs it, in the open, and it costs you nothing. That is the whole offer.
  </p>

  <h2>Why it exists</h2>
  <p>
    Twente is not short of technology. The University of Twente and Saxion have produced
    <a href="https://www.utwente.nl/business/over-de-regio-twente/" rel="noopener"
      >around 2,000 spin-offs in recent years, together accounting for some 20,000 jobs</a
    >. What is missing is not activity but visibility: the meetups that exist are organised by
    speciality, so a tester and an embedded engineer on the same street structurally never meet. And
    anyone new here — a student, someone who just moved, someone changing jobs — has almost nowhere
    to see who nearby does the same kind of work.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is a findability problem, not a shortage of people. Which is why twente.dev is not another
    specialist club but the place in between them.
  </p>

  <h2>What you will find here</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>A shared calendar.</strong> Tech events from across the region in one place, with a feed
      you can subscribe to. Other groups' events included, always linking back to their own page.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>An evening with no speciality.</strong> A few times a year, one edition that does not revolve
      around a single language or framework — <a href={routePath('edition001', 'en')}
        >twente.dev/001</a
      > is the first.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>An archive.</strong> Field reports from practice, written by people who work here. Once
      published, it stays up.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Who runs it and who pays</h2>
  <p>
    twente.dev is run by one person: Ryan Grippeling. There is no foundation and no board, and there
    will not be one for now — at this scale that would be paperwork impersonating governance.
  </p>
  <p>
    Ryan funds it himself and runs it through his own sole proprietorship, Webgrip. His employer,
    Code14, contributes time, a small marketing budget and the room for the first two editions. That
    is the entire list. Neither of them buys speaking time, attendee data or influence over what
    gets published here — least of all them, precisely because they are closest to it. After every
    edition a public write-up appears, with a financial summary and with what did not work.
  </p>

  <h2>What we do not do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>No paid speaking slots. Who gets on stage is decided by the open call.</li>
    <li>No sharing of attendee lists. With anyone, in any form.</li>
    <li>No product pitches dressed up as knowledge sharing.</li>
    <li>
      No pretending the existing meetups have failed. They keep their own identity, their own list
      and their own stage; <a href={routePath('communities', 'en')}
        >we only make them easier to find</a
      >, and only if they want that.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Dutch and English, both for real</h2>
  <p>
    A good share of the people building technology here arrived through the University of Twente and
    do not (yet) speak Dutch. Events default to Dutch and switch to English the moment there are
    internationals in the room. The site exists fully in both languages, and where an article exists
    in only one we say so plainly rather than machine-translating it.
  </p>

  <h2>Why you can check all this</h2>
  <p>
    Every event, every community listing and every article is a file in a
    <a href={REPO_URL}>public repository</a>. You can see when something was added, by whom, and
    what changed since — including this page. The site sets no cookies and loads no third-party
    scripts, so there is no cookie banner either; what we do collect is on the
    <a href={routePath('privacy', 'en')}>privacy page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Taking part</h2>
  <p>
    The community grows through contributions: a half-hour story, a field report, a room, an event
    you know about. Start at <a href={routePath('contribute', 'en')}>contribute</a>, or mail
    <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>. One sentence is enough to start.
  </p>
</ProsePage>
